1
00:00:04,438 --> 00:00:09,559
What purpose I had in view when I was hot on tracing out and proving Estella’s parentage,

2
00:00:09,559 --> 00:00:13,917
I cannot say. It will presently be seen that the question was not

3
00:00:13,917 --> 00:00:21,160
before me in a distinct shape until it was put before me by a wiser head than my own.

4
00:00:21,160 --> 00:00:26,358
But when Herbert and I had held our momentous conversation, I was seized with a feverish

5
00:00:26,358 --> 00:00:32,000
conviction that I ought to hunt the matter down,—that I ought not to let it rest,

6
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:38,078
but that I ought to see Mr. Jaggers, and come at the bare truth. I really do not know whether

7
00:00:38,078 --> 00:00:42,957
I felt that I did this for Estella’s sake, or whether I was glad to transfer to the man in

8
00:00:42,957 --> 00:00:48,000
whose preservation I was so much concerned some rays of the romantic interest that had

9
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:54,680
so long surrounded me. Perhaps the latter possibility may be the nearer to the truth.

10
00:00:54,680 --> 00:01:00,559
Any way, I could scarcely be withheld from going out to Gerrard Street that night.

11
00:01:00,559 --> 00:01:07,000
Herbert’s representations that, if I did, I should probably be laid up and stricken useless, when our

12
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:13,277
fugitive’s safety would depend upon me, alone restrained my impatience. On the understanding,

13
00:01:13,277 --> 00:01:19,000
again and again reiterated, that, come what would, I was to go to Mr. Jaggers to-morrow,

14
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:23,597
I at length submitted to keep quiet, and to have my hurts looked after, and to stay at

15
00:01:23,597 --> 00:01:30,277
home. Early next morning we went out together, and at the corner of Giltspur Street by Smithfield,

16
00:01:30,277 --> 00:01:34,879
I left Herbert to go his way into the City, and took my way to Little Britain.

17
00:01:34,879 --> 00:01:39,277
There were periodical occasions when Mr. Jaggers and Wemmick went over the office accounts,

18
00:01:39,277 --> 00:01:44,277
and checked off the vouchers, and put all things straight. On these occasions,

19
00:01:44,277 --> 00:01:49,319
Wemmick took his books and papers into Mr. Jaggers’s room, and one of the upstairs clerks

20
00:01:49,319 --> 00:01:54,879
came down into the outer office. Finding such clerk on Wemmick’s post that morning,

21
00:01:54,879 --> 00:02:00,438
I knew what was going on; but I was not sorry to have Mr. Jaggers and Wemmick together,

22
00:02:00,438 --> 00:02:05,000
as Wemmick would then hear for himself that I said nothing to compromise him.

23
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:12,078
My appearance, with my arm bandaged and my coat loose over my shoulders, favoured my object.

24
00:02:12,078 --> 00:02:17,160
Although I had sent Mr. Jaggers a brief account of the accident as soon as I had arrived in town,

25
00:02:17,160 --> 00:02:21,957
yet I had to give him all the details now; and the speciality of the occasion caused

26
00:02:21,957 --> 00:02:27,758
our talk to be less dry and hard, and less strictly regulated by the rules of evidence,

27
00:02:27,758 --> 00:02:34,398
than it had been before. While I described the disaster, Mr. Jaggers stood, according

28
00:02:34,398 --> 00:02:41,718
to his wont, before the fire. Wemmick leaned back in his chair, staring at me,

29
00:02:41,718 --> 00:02:47,358
with his hands in the pockets of his trousers, and his pen put horizontally into the post.

30
00:02:47,358 --> 00:02:52,557
The two brutal casts, always inseparable in my mind from the official proceedings,

31
00:02:52,559 --> 00:02:59,264
seemed to be congestively considering whether they didn’t smell fire at the present moment.

32
00:02:59,264 --> 00:03:01,957
My narrative finished, and their questions exhausted,

33
00:03:01,957 --> 00:03:09,038
I then produced Miss Havisham’s authority to receive the nine hundred pounds for Herbert.

34
00:03:09,038 --> 00:03:14,199
Mr. Jaggers’s eyes retired a little deeper into his head when I handed him the tablets,

35
00:03:14,199 --> 00:03:20,358
but he presently handed them over to Wemmick, with instructions to draw the check for his signature.

36
00:03:20,358 --> 00:03:26,116
While that was in course of being done, I looked on at Wemmick as he wrote, and Mr. Jaggers,

37
00:03:26,116 --> 00:03:32,480
poising and swaying himself on his well-polished boots, looked on at me. “I am sorry, Pip,” said

38
00:03:32,480 --> 00:03:37,557
he, as I put the check in my pocket, when he had signed it, “that we do nothing for you.”

39
00:03:37,559 --> 00:03:40,480
“Miss Havisham was good enough to ask me,” I returned,

40
00:03:40,480 --> 00:03:45,078
“whether she could do nothing for me, and I told her No.”

41
00:03:45,078 --> 00:03:47,680
“Everybody should know his own business,” said Mr.

42
00:03:47,680 --> 00:03:52,118
Jaggers. And I saw Wemmick’s lips form the words “portable property.”

43
00:03:53,796 --> 00:03:57,559
“I should not have told her No, if I had been you,” said Mr Jaggers;

44
00:03:57,559 --> 00:04:00,917
“but every man ought to know his own business best.”

45
00:04:00,917 --> 00:04:06,718
“Every man’s business,” said Wemmick, rather reproachfully towards me, “is portable property.”

46
00:04:06,718 --> 00:04:10,718
As I thought the time was now come for pursuing the theme I had at heart,

47
00:04:10,718 --> 00:04:13,358
I said, turning on Mr. Jaggers:—

48
00:04:13,358 --> 00:04:17,557
“I did ask something of Miss Havisham, however, sir. I asked her to give me

49
00:04:17,559 --> 00:04:22,118
some information relative to her adopted daughter, and she gave me all she possessed.”

50
00:04:23,038 --> 00:04:27,277
“Did she?” said Mr. Jaggers, bending forward to look at his boots and

51
00:04:27,277 --> 00:04:32,237
then straightening himself. “Hah! I don’t think I should have done so,

52
00:04:32,237 --> 00:04:37,957
if I had been Miss Havisham. But she ought to know her own business best.”

53
00:04:37,957 --> 00:04:41,839
“I know more of the history of Miss Havisham’s adopted child than Miss

54
00:04:41,839 --> 00:04:46,358
Havisham herself does, sir. I know her mother.”

55
00:04:46,358 --> 00:04:51,398
Mr. Jaggers looked at me inquiringly, and repeated “Mother?”

56
00:04:51,398 --> 00:04:54,838
“I have seen her mother within these three days.”

57
00:04:54,839 --> 00:04:57,358
“Yes?” said Mr. Jaggers.

58
00:04:57,358 --> 00:05:01,277
“And so have you, sir. And you have seen her still more recently.”

59
00:05:01,277 --> 00:05:03,678
“Yes?” said Mr. Jaggers.

60
00:05:03,680 --> 00:05:10,559
“Perhaps I know more of Estella’s history than even you do,” said I. “I know her father too.”

61
00:05:10,559 --> 00:05:15,917
A certain stop that Mr. Jaggers came to in his manner—he was too self-possessed to change his

62
00:05:15,917 --> 00:05:22,160
manner, but he could not help its being brought to an indefinably attentive stop—assured me

63
00:05:22,160 --> 00:05:28,000
that he did not know who her father was. This I had strongly suspected from Provis’s account (as

64
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:33,637
Herbert had repeated it) of his having kept himself dark; which I pieced on to the fact

65
00:05:33,637 --> 00:05:38,879
that he himself was not Mr. Jaggers’s client until some four years later,

66
00:05:38,879 --> 00:05:44,038
and when he could have no reason for claiming his identity. But, I could not be sure of

67
00:05:44,038 --> 00:05:50,758
this unconsciousness on Mr. Jaggers’s part before, though I was quite sure of it now.

68
00:05:50,758 --> 00:05:56,358
“So! You know the young lady’s father, Pip?” said Mr. Jaggers.

69
00:05:56,358 --> 00:06:02,358
“Yes,” I replied, “and his name is Provis—from New South Wales.”

70
00:06:02,358 --> 00:06:07,436
Even Mr. Jaggers started when I said those words. It was the slightest start that could

71
00:06:07,438 --> 00:06:13,078
escape a man, the most carefully repressed and the sooner checked, but he did start,

72
00:06:13,078 --> 00:06:20,758
though he made it a part of the action of taking out his pocket-handkerchief. How Wemmick received

73
00:06:20,758 --> 00:06:27,199
the announcement I am unable to say; for I was afraid to look at him just then, lest Mr.

74
00:06:27,199 --> 00:06:34,557
Jaggers’s sharpness should detect that there had been some communication unknown to him between us.

75
00:06:34,559 --> 00:06:38,758
“And on what evidence, Pip,” asked Mr. Jaggers, very coolly,

76
00:06:38,758 --> 00:06:45,000
as he paused with his handkerchief half way to his nose, “does Provis make this claim?”

77
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:48,917
“He does not make it,” said I, “and has never made it,

78
00:06:48,917 --> 00:06:51,917
and has no knowledge or belief that his daughter is in existence.”

79
00:06:53,438 --> 00:06:59,758
For once, the powerful pocket-handkerchief failed. My reply was so unexpected, that

80
00:06:59,758 --> 00:07:04,758
Mr. Jaggers put the handkerchief back into his pocket without completing the usual performance,

81
00:07:04,758 --> 00:07:10,680
folded his arms, and looked with stern attention at me, though with an immovable face.

82
00:07:10,680 --> 00:07:15,199
Then I told him all I knew, and how I knew it; with the one reservation that I left

83
00:07:15,199 --> 00:07:21,078
him to infer that I knew from Miss Havisham what I in fact knew from Wemmick. I was very

84
00:07:21,078 --> 00:07:27,000
careful indeed as to that. Nor did I look towards Wemmick until I had finished all I had to tell,

85
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:33,319
and had been for some time silently meeting Mr. Jaggers’s look. When I did at last turn my eyes in

86
00:07:33,319 --> 00:07:40,917
Wemmick’s direction, I found that he had unposted his pen, and was intent upon the table before him.

87
00:07:40,917 --> 00:07:47,237
“Hah!” said Mr. Jaggers at last, as he moved towards the papers on the table.

88
00:07:47,237 --> 00:07:51,839
“What item was it you were at, Wemmick, when Mr. Pip came in?”

89
00:07:52,718 --> 00:07:57,839
But I could not submit to be thrown off in that way, and I made a passionate, almost

90
00:07:57,839 --> 00:08:04,277
an indignant appeal, to him to be more frank and manly with me. I reminded him of the false hopes

91
00:08:04,277 --> 00:08:10,038
into which I had lapsed, the length of time they had lasted, and the discovery I had made:

92
00:08:10,038 --> 00:08:16,358
and I hinted at the danger that weighed upon my spirits. I represented myself as being surely

93
00:08:16,358 --> 00:08:21,838
worthy of some little confidence from him, in return for the confidence I had just now imparted.

94
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:28,718
I said that I did not blame him, or suspect him, or mistrust him, but I wanted assurance of the

95
00:08:28,718 --> 00:08:35,038
truth from him. And if he asked me why I wanted it, and why I thought I had any right to it,

96
00:08:35,038 --> 00:08:41,519
I would tell him, little as he cared for such poor dreams, that I had loved Estella dearly and long,

97
00:08:41,519 --> 00:08:46,879
and that although I had lost her, and must live a bereaved life, whatever concerned her

98
00:08:46,879 --> 00:08:53,319
was still nearer and dearer to me than anything else in the world. And seeing that Mr. Jaggers

99
00:08:53,319 --> 00:08:59,917
stood quite still and silent, and apparently quite obdurate, under this appeal, I turned to Wemmick,

100
00:08:59,917 --> 00:09:06,559
and said, “Wemmick, I know you to be a man with a gentle heart. I have seen your pleasant home,

101
00:09:06,559 --> 00:09:11,398
and your old father, and all the innocent, cheerful playful ways with which you refresh

102
00:09:11,398 --> 00:09:16,838
your business life. And I entreat you to say a word for me to Mr. Jaggers,

103
00:09:16,839 --> 00:09:21,758
and to represent to him that, all circumstances considered, he ought to be more open with me!”

104
00:09:23,438 --> 00:09:26,199
I have never seen two men look more oddly at one

105
00:09:26,199 --> 00:09:31,957
another than Mr. Jaggers and Wemmick did after this apostrophe. At first,

106
00:09:31,957 --> 00:09:37,078
a misgiving crossed me that Wemmick would be instantly dismissed from his employment;

107
00:09:37,078 --> 00:09:45,277
but it melted as I saw Mr. Jaggers relax into something like a smile, and Wemmick become bolder.

108
00:09:45,277 --> 00:09:49,116
“What’s all this?” said Mr. Jaggers. “You with an old father,

109
00:09:49,116 --> 00:09:52,078
and you with pleasant and playful ways?”

110
00:09:52,078 --> 00:09:57,160
“Well!” returned Wemmick. “If I don’t bring ’em here, what does it matter?”

111
00:09:57,160 --> 00:10:02,038
“Pip,” said Mr. Jaggers, laying his hand upon my arm, and smiling openly,

112
00:10:02,038 --> 00:10:06,277
“this man must be the most cunning impostor in all London.”

113
00:10:06,277 --> 00:10:12,319
“Not a bit of it,” returned Wemmick, growing bolder and bolder. “I think you’re another.”

114
00:10:12,319 --> 00:10:14,796
Again they exchanged their former odd looks,

115
00:10:14,796 --> 00:10:18,758
each apparently still distrustful that the other was taking him in.

116
00:10:18,758 --> 00:10:21,718
“You with a pleasant home?” said Mr. Jaggers.

117
00:10:22,319 --> 00:10:27,000
“Since it don’t interfere with business,” returned Wemmick, “let it be so. Now,

118
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:30,879
I look at you, sir, I shouldn’t wonder if you might be planning and contriving to

119
00:10:30,879 --> 00:10:36,718
have a pleasant home of your own one of these days, when you’re tired of all this work.”

120
00:10:36,718 --> 00:10:41,957
Mr. Jaggers nodded his head retrospectively two or three times, and actually drew a

121
00:10:41,957 --> 00:10:51,160
sigh. “Pip,” said he, “we won’t talk about ‘poor dreams;’ you know more about such things than I,

122
00:10:51,160 --> 00:10:57,319
having much fresher experience of that kind. But now about this other matter. I’ll put a

123
00:10:57,319 --> 00:11:02,358
case to you. Mind! I admit nothing.” He waited for me to declare that I

124
00:11:02,358 --> 00:11:06,277
quite understood that he expressly said that he admitted nothing.

125
00:11:06,277 --> 00:11:11,839
“Now, Pip,” said Mr. Jaggers, “put this case. Put the case that a woman,

126
00:11:11,839 --> 00:11:17,160
under such circumstances as you have mentioned, held her child concealed, and was obliged to

127
00:11:17,160 --> 00:11:23,038
communicate the fact to her legal adviser, on his representing to her that he must know, with

128
00:11:23,038 --> 00:11:29,917
an eye to the latitude of his defence, how the fact stood about that child. Put the case that,

129
00:11:29,917 --> 00:11:36,559
at the same time he held a trust to find a child for an eccentric rich lady to adopt and bring up.”

130
00:11:36,559 --> 00:11:38,597
“I follow you, sir.”

131
00:11:38,597 --> 00:11:42,277
“Put the case that he lived in an atmosphere of evil,

132
00:11:42,277 --> 00:11:47,519
and that all he saw of children was their being generated in great numbers for certain

133
00:11:47,519 --> 00:11:54,358
destruction. Put the case that he often saw children solemnly tried at a criminal bar,

134
00:11:54,358 --> 00:12:00,116
where they were held up to be seen; put the case that he habitually knew of their being imprisoned,

135
00:12:00,116 --> 00:12:06,160
whipped, transported, neglected, cast out, qualified in all ways for the hangman,

136
00:12:06,160 --> 00:12:11,879
and growing up to be hanged. Put the case that pretty nigh all the children he saw in his daily

137
00:12:11,879 --> 00:12:17,319
business life he had reason to look upon as so much spawn, to develop into the fish that were

138
00:12:17,319 --> 00:12:25,758
to come to his net,—to be prosecuted, defended, forsworn, made orphans, bedevilled somehow.”

139
00:12:25,758 --> 00:12:27,680
“I follow you, sir.”

140
00:12:27,680 --> 00:12:33,160
“Put the case, Pip, that here was one pretty little child out of the heap who could be saved;

141
00:12:33,160 --> 00:12:39,319
whom the father believed dead, and dared make no stir about; as to whom, over the mother,

142
00:12:39,319 --> 00:12:46,078
the legal adviser had this power: “I know what you did, and how you did it. You came so and so,

143
00:12:46,078 --> 00:12:51,277
you did such and such things to divert suspicion. I have tracked you through it all,

144
00:12:51,277 --> 00:12:58,078
and I tell it you all. Part with the child, unless it should be necessary to produce it to clear you,

145
00:12:58,078 --> 00:13:02,519
and then it shall be produced. Give the child into my hands,

146
00:13:02,519 --> 00:13:10,237
and I will do my best to bring you off. If you are saved, your child is saved too; if you are lost,

147
00:13:10,237 --> 00:13:16,917
your child is still saved.” Put the case that this was done, and that the woman was cleared.”

148
00:13:16,917 --> 00:13:19,398
“I understand you perfectly.”

149
00:13:19,398 --> 00:13:21,877
“But that I make no admissions?”

150
00:13:21,879 --> 00:13:28,038
“That you make no admissions.” And Wemmick repeated, “No admissions.”

151
00:13:28,038 --> 00:13:33,917
“Put the case, Pip, that passion and the terror of death had a little shaken the woman’s intellects,

152
00:13:33,917 --> 00:13:38,319
and that when she was set at liberty, she was scared out of the ways of the world,

153
00:13:38,319 --> 00:13:44,879
and went to him to be sheltered. Put the case that he took her in, and that he kept down the old,

154
00:13:44,879 --> 00:13:49,438
wild, violent nature whenever he saw an inkling of its breaking out,

155
00:13:49,438 --> 00:13:56,237
by asserting his power over her in the old way. Do you comprehend the imaginary case?”

156
00:13:56,237 --> 00:13:57,957
“Quite.”

157
00:13:57,957 --> 00:14:02,839
“Put the case that the child grew up, and was married for money. That the

158
00:14:02,839 --> 00:14:08,597
mother was still living. That the father was still living. That the mother and father,

159
00:14:08,597 --> 00:14:13,637
unknown to one another, were dwelling within so many miles, furlongs,

160
00:14:13,637 --> 00:14:18,237
yards if you like, of one another. That the secret was still a secret,

161
00:14:18,237 --> 00:14:23,678
except that you had got wind of it. Put that last case to yourself very carefully.”

162
00:14:23,680 --> 00:14:25,116
“I do.”

163
00:14:25,116 --> 00:14:28,358
“I ask Wemmick to put it to himself very carefully.”

164
00:14:28,358 --> 00:14:31,557
And Wemmick said, “I do.”

165
00:14:31,559 --> 00:14:36,796
“For whose sake would you reveal the secret? For the father’s? I think he would not be much the

166
00:14:36,796 --> 00:14:42,637
better for the mother. For the mother’s? I think if she had done such a deed she would be safer

167
00:14:42,637 --> 00:14:47,597
where she was. For the daughter’s? I think it would hardly serve her to establish her

168
00:14:47,597 --> 00:14:53,319
parentage for the information of her husband, and to drag her back to disgrace, after an escape of

169
00:14:53,319 --> 00:14:59,160
twenty years, pretty secure to last for life. But add the case that you had loved her, Pip,

170
00:14:59,160 --> 00:15:04,199
and had made her the subject of those ‘poor dreams’ which have, at one time or another,

171
00:15:04,199 --> 00:15:10,319
been in the heads of more men than you think likely, then I tell you that you had better—and

172
00:15:10,319 --> 00:15:15,557
would much sooner when you had thought well of it—chop off that bandaged left hand of yours

173
00:15:15,559 --> 00:15:22,796
with your bandaged right hand, and then pass the chopper on to Wemmick there, to cut that off too.”

174
00:15:22,796 --> 00:15:28,199
I looked at Wemmick, whose face was very grave. He gravely touched his lips with

175
00:15:28,199 --> 00:15:36,199
his forefinger. I did the same. Mr. Jaggers did the same. “Now, Wemmick,” said the latter then,

176
00:15:36,199 --> 00:15:42,917
resuming his usual manner, “what item was it you were at when Mr. Pip came in?”

177
00:15:42,917 --> 00:15:46,160
Standing by for a little, while they were at work,

178
00:15:46,160 --> 00:15:51,319
I observed that the odd looks they had cast at one another were repeated several times:

179
00:15:52,277 --> 00:15:57,597
with this difference now, that each of them seemed suspicious, not to say conscious, of

180
00:15:57,597 --> 00:16:04,237
having shown himself in a weak and unprofessional light to the other. For this reason, I suppose,

181
00:16:04,237 --> 00:16:10,678
they were now inflexible with one another; Mr. Jaggers being highly dictatorial, and Wemmick

182
00:16:10,680 --> 00:16:16,519
obstinately justifying himself whenever there was the smallest point in abeyance for a moment.

183
00:16:16,519 --> 00:16:21,321
I had never seen them on such ill terms; for generally they got on very well indeed together.

184
00:16:22,116 --> 00:16:27,480
But they were both happily relieved by the opportune appearance of Mike, the client with the

185
00:16:27,480 --> 00:16:33,398
fur cap and the habit of wiping his nose on his sleeve, whom I had seen on the very first day of

186
00:16:33,398 --> 00:16:40,877
my appearance within those walls. This individual, who, either in his own person or in that of some

187
00:16:40,879 --> 00:16:46,519
member of his family, seemed to be always in trouble (which in that place meant Newgate),

188
00:16:46,519 --> 00:16:51,239
called to announce that his eldest daughter was taken up on suspicion of shoplifting.

189
00:16:52,480 --> 00:16:58,116
As he imparted this melancholy circumstance to Wemmick, Mr. Jaggers standing magisterially before

190
00:16:58,116 --> 00:17:05,116
the fire and taking no share in the proceedings, Mike’s eye happened to twinkle with a tear.

191
00:17:05,116 --> 00:17:07,319
“What are you about?” demanded Wemmick,

192
00:17:07,319 --> 00:17:11,796
with the utmost indignation. “What do you come snivelling here for?”

193
00:17:11,796 --> 00:17:14,000
“I didn’t go to do it, Mr. Wemmick.”

194
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:18,718
“You did,” said Wemmick. “How dare you? You’re not in a fit state to come here,

195
00:17:18,718 --> 00:17:23,519
if you can’t come here without spluttering like a bad pen. What do you mean by it?”

196
00:17:23,519 --> 00:17:27,160
“A man can’t help his feelings, Mr. Wemmick,” pleaded Mike.

197
00:17:27,160 --> 00:17:32,436
“His what?” demanded Wemmick, quite savagely. “Say that again!”

198
00:17:32,438 --> 00:17:35,199
“Now look here my man,” said Mr. Jaggers, advancing a step,

199
00:17:35,199 --> 00:17:40,277
and pointing to the door. “Get out of this office. I’ll have no feelings here. Get out.”

200
00:17:40,277 --> 00:17:43,917
“It serves you right,” said Wemmick, “Get out.”

201
00:17:43,917 --> 00:17:49,319
So, the unfortunate Mike very humbly withdrew, and Mr. Jaggers and Wemmick appeared to have

202
00:17:49,319 --> 00:17:54,480
re-established their good understanding, and went to work again with an air of refreshment upon them

203
00:17:54,480 --> 00:17:57,000
as if they had just had lunch. Chapter 52.
